{"problem_sha256":"f76b3e036f1d63cc9d36ff3f18f497d6365f26dfb3faad836c262ab391ed8754","input_sha256":"774e302ef0a14bebf38b2c3fc78c3e0d55fadec4a806742c8325ece49e36569f","read_receipts":[{"object_id":"obj_2808dc0cd802e9efefc9f3bf9c149628f420df618eeea1e512c0efda43b0aa94","sha256":"86c4f134dcfa3d1ddf9df7da59efa1c04b5a26cc054a00d786d0ce88f7f18a5a","media_type":"application/json","size_bytes":7651},{"object_id":"obj_6e2487cc4ef2154868551f66ea7847b70ddc230d61adda48d03ed478be5f1442","sha256":"935ff1681eba45b0f9990b71a05632fac036375339cc004a000e14c49905c592","media_type":"text/markdown","size_bytes":5225}],"usage":{"provider_requests":null,"total_tokens":null},"output":{"lemma_proposals":[{"statement":"Let m,n be coprime integers with n>0, mn≠0, and m²≠n². Set H=m²+n², D=m²−n², and Q=m⁴+34m²n²+n⁴. Define a=−9H/(8D), b=(m²−7n²)H(7m²−n²)/(8D³), c=8D³/(9H³), e=−2(m²+5n²)(5m²+n²)/(9DH), f=−2mn(m²−4mn−3n²)(3m²−4mn−n²)(m³+8m²n+5mn²+4n³)(4m³−5m²n+8mn²−n³)/(DHQ²), and g=2mn(m²+4mn−3n²)(3m²+4mn−n²)(m³−8m²n+5mn²−4n³)(4m³+5m²n+8mn²+n³)/(DHQ²). Then none of a,b,c,e,f,g is an integer. The positive integer Q has a prime divisor p>3; every prime p>3 dividing Q satisfies p≡1 (mod 8) and vₚ(f)=vₚ(g)=−2vₚ(Q), where vₚ is the normalized p-adic valuation.","assumptions":["m,n are coprime integers with n>0, mn≠0, and m²≠n².","H,D,Q and the six rational numbers a,b,c,e,f,g are exactly as defined in the statement."],"quantifiers":["For every pair of integers m,n satisfying the assumptions, without a height bound.","For every prime p>3 dividing the corresponding Q."],"proof":"First, m²−7n² and 7m²−n² are nonzero: if either vanished, a reduced rational square would equal 7 or 1/7, respectively, which is impossible by comparing the exponent of 7. Since gcd(m,n)=1, either m,n have opposite parity or both are odd. In the opposite-parity case, H,D and both of these quadratic factors are odd, so v₂(a)=v₂(b)=−3. If both are odd, then v₂(H)=1 and v₂(D)≥3, while m²−7n²≡2 and 7m²−n²≡6 (mod 8), so each quadratic factor has 2-adic valuation 1. Thus v₂(a)=1−3−v₂(D)<0 and v₂(b)=1+1+1−3−3v₂(D)=−3v₂(D)<0. In either case a,b are nonintegers. Also H+D=2m²>0 and H−D=2n²>0, so 0<|D|<H and 0<|c|=(8/9)(|D|/H)³<8/9. Hence c is not an integer.

Write N=−2(m²+5n²)(5m²+n²), the numerator of e. If e were an integer, then H would divide N. Modulo H, m²≡−n², whence N≡32n⁴ (mod H). Because gcd(H,n)=1, this implies H|32. The 2-adic valuation of H is 0 for opposite parity and 1 when both are odd. A positive divisor of 32 with either valuation is at most 2. But |m| and n are distinct positive integers, so H=m²+n²≥5. This contradiction proves e is not an integer.

The integer Q is positive. In the opposite-parity case it is odd. If both m,n are odd, m⁴≡n⁴≡1 (mod 16), and m²n²≡1 or 9 (mod 16); as 34≡2 (mod 16), this gives Q≡4 (mod 16). Thus v₂(Q) is 0 or 2. If 3|mn, coprimality gives Q≡1 (mod 3). Otherwise m²≡n²≡1 (mod 3), so D=3k for some integer k. The identity Q=D²+36m²n² gives Q=9(k²+4m²n²); the parenthetical factor is nonzero modulo 3, since k² is 0 or 1 and 4m²n²≡1 modulo 3. Hence v₃(Q) is 0 or 2. If Q had no prime divisor greater than 3, these bounds would imply Q|36. But at least one of |m|,n is at least 2 and the other at least 1, so Q=m⁴+n⁴+34m²n²≥16+1+136=153. Therefore Q has a prime divisor p>3.

Fix any such p. No prime dividing Q divides m or n: reducing Q modulo a prime dividing either would leave the nonzero fourth power of the other, by coprimality. Thus p∤mn. Also Q=D²+36m²n² and Q=H²+32m²n² show p∤D and p∤H: if p divided D or H, the respective identity and p>3, p∤mn would give a contradiction.

To rule out cancellation in the numerator of f, put q(T)=T⁴+34T²+1 and
P₁(T)=T²−4T−3, P₂(T)=3T²−4T−1, P₃(T)=T³+8T²+5T+4, P₄(T)=4T³−5T²+8T−1.
The following identities in Z[T] hold:
q+(-T³−34T)T=1;
(33−7T)q+(7T³−5T²+239T−181)P₁=576;
(197−123T)q+(41T³−11T²+1393T−379)P₂=576;
(17T²+123T−20)q+(-17T³+13T²−577T+437)P₃=1728;
(628T²−717T+1151)q+(-157T³−17T²−5333T−577)P₄=1728.
These identities follow by multiplying and collecting coefficients. In the field Fₚ set T=m/n, which is defined since p∤n. Then q(T)=Q/n⁴=0. The first identity implies T≠0. Since p>3, 576 and 1728 are nonzero modulo p; the other identities therefore imply Pᵢ(T)≠0 for i=1,2,3,4. Homogenizing P₁,P₂ multiplies their evaluations by n², and homogenizing P₃,P₄ multiplies theirs by n³; these are units modulo p. Consequently every factor in the numerator of f, including mn, is a p-adic unit. The denominator DHQ² has p-adic valuation 2vₚ(Q), since p∤DH. Therefore vₚ(f)=−2vₚ(Q)<0, so f is not an integer.

Substituting (−m,n) into f gives g(m,n): the prefactor changes sign, the quadratic factors become those of g, and each cubic factor becomes the negative of its corresponding cubic factor in g, so the two cubic signs cancel. The pair (−m,n) still satisfies the assumptions, and H,D,Q are unchanged. Applying the preceding argument to (−m,n) at the same p proves vₚ(g)=−2vₚ(Q)<0. Thus g is not an integer, and the valuation assertions hold for every p>3 dividing Q.

Finally, in Fₚ set r=D/(6mn) and s=H/(4mn); their denominators are units. The two identities for Q give r²=−1 and s²=−2. With u=s/r we have u²=2. Put z=u(1+r)/2. Then z²=2(1+2r+r²)/4=r, and therefore z⁴=−1. Since p is odd, −1≠1, so z⁴≠1 and z⁸=1; hence z has order exactly 8 in Fₚ*. Its order divides p−1, proving p≡1 (mod 8). This result is confined to the displayed rational family and does not resolve the broader two-integer sextuple existence question.","dependency_ids":[],"source_candidate_id":"cand_42107f440fe44de18e5df9b72a21e064"}]}}
